  "sourceFindings": [
    "CPU_Core_Dynrec_Run checks the code-page cache and calls CreateCacheBlock only when no block is found; up to 32 opcodes per translation. Linked existing blocks execute generated code without re-decoding their instruction streams.",
    "CodePageHandler write handlers invalidate affected translated ranges on changed code bytes; identical writes are already ignored. Cache reuse/eviction can also require translation; the metadata does not encode the reason for each clear.",
    "Frequently modified instruction starts and unsupported/special instructions use the normal interpreter fallback; measured interpreter time includes execution, not just fetching.",
    "decode_fetch*_imm already supports reading some frequently modified immediates through guest-memory pointers where a usable TLB entry exists. Removing invalidation or caching mutable code blindly would change guest behavior.",
    "The guest snapshot contains explicit stores into nearby executable immediates, for example instruction 0x2b511b writes operand storage at 0x2b516c. This confirms self-modifying rendering code exists; it does not prove the reason for every recorded retranslation."
  ],
  "decision": "No instruction-fetch optimization justified by this profile. Fetch-attributed work is small; even the broader translation and interpreter paths are only a small share. Preserve required reads/invalidation. Keep the separately measured MakeCodePage cost for #62, and investigate data accesses in #59.",
  "limitations": [
    "CPU sampling and optimized DWARF attribution are estimates, not an exact count of fetches or a proof of zero cost elsewhere. Incomplete call chains and unresolved PCs remain.",
    "Inclusive category totals overlap and must not be summed with their contained fetch costs. Translation includes code emission; interpreter includes instruction execution.",
    "The 48,731 translation events come from the separate #55 instrumented capture. Its measured overhead is documented in #55; these counts are not normal-build execution throughput.",
    "Repeated guest starts do not identify invalidation cause or establish a cache bug. Guest opcode-start counts include retranslations and are not executed-instruction counts.",
    "This decision covers the measured dynamic-core workload, not every interpreter-heavy or self-modifying game."
  ],
